  "tasks": [
    {
      "id": "448a9630-74e6-4fd3-a2f2-3e257fedd1eb",
      "phaseId": "99d19246-1ba9-4720-9895-e70c66acf144",
      "number": 347,
      "shortId": "FUC7F",
      "priority": 20,
      "shortName": "chrome-keyboard-focus-is-a-reg",
      "title": "Focus a dock by picking it: FocusDock through ActionRegistry + KeyHint",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "# The bug this replaces\n\nA sidebar is a **container** and left/right is a position. `handle_sidebar_focus`\n(`heca/src/handlers.rs:1532`) reads `left_visible()` and calls `set_left_mode(Expanded)` — it names a\nside. The workspaces dock declares `RegionSet::sidebars()`, so it may be seated on the right\n(`heca/src/providers/workspaces.rs:66`), and then the focus key **expands the empty left container\nand navigates a tree drawn on the right**. `state.sidebar_tree` carries no region either.\n\nThere is also no notion of chrome keyboard focus at all, and `Provider` has no focus concept\n(`heca/src/providers/mod.rs`: id, supported_regions, default_region, default_order, title, movable,\ncollapsible, grow, build_contribution, context_menus, on_activate).\n\n# ✅ DECIDED BY THE USER 2026-07-28 — pick a dock, through the existing machinery\n\n**`FocusDock`: an action that hints every dock and focuses the one picked.** Built out of what is\nalready there, not a new subsystem:\n\n- **`ActionRegistry`** owns the action, with its descriptor and its declared args (F003/P010/T006).\n- **`KeymapRegistry`** binds it, so the key is configurable and never hardcoded — and because it is\n  an action, **RPC can call it** like any other.\n- **`KeyHint`** draws the letters. The workspaces dock already wraps each workspace in one\n  (`KeyHint::new(dock).hint(..).placement(TopRight)`, `providers/workspaces.rs`), so a dock pick is\n  the same idiom one level up.\n\n## The shape\n\n1. **`WmAction::FocusDock { dock: Option<ContainerId> }`** — one action, two ways in:\n   - **bare** (`focus_dock`) → enters the pick: every mounted container lights a letter, press one\n     and it takes focus. This is the keybinding case.\n   - **with an id** (`focus_dock(dock=\"workspaces\")`) → focuses it directly, no pick. This is the RPC\n     and scripting case, and it falls out of the optional-argument machinery for free.\n   Declared as `ArgDescriptor::optional_*`, so the bare binding stays legal (an action that *requires*\n   an argument cannot carry one).\n2. **Candidates come from the host**, not from a side: `ChromeHost::contributions(region)` over the\n   regions, in the order the host maintains. Letters assigned as the other picks do\n   (`pick_candidates` / `PendingPick`, `heca/src/chrome/state.rs`).\n3. **Focus is state: a container id.** Not a side, not a dock's name written in code. Sticky, as\n   focus is.\n4. **Focus must be visible.** A focused container needs a ring or marker, or nothing tells the user\n   where the keyboard is. This is a visual decision → the user judges it in the app.\n5. **`Provider::keyboard_navigable()`** (default `false`) says whether a dock does anything with\n   focus beyond scrolling. `WorkspacesContainerProvider` returns `true`. Knowledge of what can be\n   navigated belongs to the provider.\n6. **The old action stops naming a side.** `sidebar_focus` becomes focus-a-dock: with one navigable\n   dock mounted it behaves as today; with none it is a no-op instead of expanding an empty container.\n\n## What it unblocks\n\n- **F003/P011/T012** app half: the scroll actions target the focused container. Its widget half is\n  shipped (`096f06e`) — every `ScrollRegion` already acts on the intents and\n  `ScrollRegion::keyboard_target(signal)` gates which one, per instance and position-agnostic. So the\n  host sends one intent and the focused container's area takes it; every other declines.\n- The dock's own navigation (the workspaces j/k cursor) follows the focused dock rather than a side.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. `Provider::keyboard_navigable()`; workspaces returns true.\n2. Chrome keyboard focus state: `Option<ContainerId>`.\n3. `WmAction::FocusDock { dock: Option<..> }` through the full Adding-New-Actions checklist\n   (AGENTS.md): variant → `action_from_name` → `action_priority` → `action_policy()` → handler →\n   `build_registry` → `keybindings.default.toml` → `ActionRegistry::ALL` descriptor **with its args\n   declaration** → `rpc.rs` → README.\n4. The pick: candidates from `ChromeHost`, letters via `KeyHint`, resolve → focus.\n5. Bind each container's `ScrollRegion::keyboard_target` to `focus == my id`.\n6. `sidebar_focus` stops naming a side.\n7. Tests: focus survives the dock being moved between regions; no navigable dock → no-op; exactly one\n   container is the keyboard target at a time; `focus_dock(dock=\"…\")` needs no pick.\n8. **USER verifies**: the letters appear over the docks, the picked one shows it has focus, and the\n   scroll keys then act on it.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- No side literal in the focus path.\n- A container's wiring names no dock.\n- `focus_dock` reachable from a keybinding **and** from RPC, the same action either way.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\n# ✅ DONE 2026-07-30 — verified in the app by the user\n\n**Closed on what it set out to do: a dock can be picked by letter and takes focus, visibly.** The user\ndrove it and confirmed: *\"FUC7F works... it brings us the hint to focus a container and it works.\"*\nWhat is deliberately **not** here — being able to *do* anything with the focused container without\nfalling back to `prefix+e` — is the whole of **P085(F007) \"Components declare their actions; chrome\nfocus is the mode\"**, opened for exactly that reason. This task is not a partial delivery of that; it\nis the state that phase builds on.\n\n## What landed\n\n- **Focus is a container id, not a side.** `SharedChromeState.focused_container` +\n  `set_focused_container` (`heca/src/chrome/state.rs`), emitting `chrome.container.focus.changed`.\n  Per-**mount** `keyboard_target` signals derived from it, so exactly one placement is the keyboard\n  target by construction, and a signal asked for after focus moved is created already knowing.\n- **`heca/src/chrome/focus.rs`** (new) — the two decisions as pure functions over `ChromeHost`:\n  `dock_candidates(host, on_screen)` (letters, host order, on-screen regions only — a letter over a\n  body nobody can see is a key that does nothing) and `navigable_dock(host, focused)` (which dock\n  `sidebar_focus` acts on **and the region it sits in**).\n- **The live bug is fixed.** `handle_sidebar_focus` no longer reads `left_visible()`; it asks the host\n  for the navigable dock, reveals *that dock's* region (`reveal_region`), focuses it, then enters nav\n  mode. With no navigable dock mounted it is a **no-op** instead of expanding an empty container.\n- **`WmAction::FocusDock { dock: Option<ContainerId> }`** through the full Adding-New-Actions\n  checklist: variant → `action_from_name` (bare ⇒ pick) → `build_action` (optional `dock` ⇒ direct) →\n  `action_priority` → `handle_focus_dock` → `build_registry` → `keybindings.default.toml`\n  (`prefix+Shift+e`) → descriptor with declared `args` → `rpc.rs` (`focus-dock [id]`) →\n  `action_policy` = `Global` (chrome state, no layout impact; `SidebarFocus` stays `TiledOnly`) →\n  README. An unknown id logs instead of vanishing.\n- **The pick**: `InputMode::DockPick` (letter → container id), resolved in `app/input.rs` back out\n  through the *same* `focus_dock` action carrying the id — the letter is only how a keyboard supplies\n  an argument it cannot type. Status bar reads `FOCUS DOCK` with the action's own prompt.\n- **`Provider::keyboard_navigable()`** (default `false`); `WorkspacesContainerProvider` returns `true`.\n- **Library**: `FocusRing` — a generic transparent wrapper drawing the theme's focus outline from a\n  host signal (a control owns its focus and rings itself; an *area* the keyboard is aimed at has no\n  owner in the tree). Same `PaintCx::focus_ring`, same tokens, respects `show_focus_border`. Plus\n  `KeyHint::new_boxed` so a dynamically built subtree can be wrapped. Both documented (rustdoc +\n  `docs/widgets.md`), showcase demo, prop-drift guard satisfied.\n- **Wiring**: `build_region_content` wraps every container in `FocusRing(KeyHint(body))`; the ring\n  binds the **same signal** the container's `ScrollRegion` takes as its `keyboard_target`, so the ring\n  and the keys cannot disagree.\n\n## The trap it hit, for the next reader\n\nThe wrappers reintroduced F003/P011/T021's failure one level down: a hugging wrapper measures its\nchild's *content* and a flex item's automatic minimum stops it shrinking back, so the dock measured\n**1214px inside its 296px slot**. `pass_box_down` passes the box through each wrapper level, and only\nwhen there **is** a share (`grow = 0.0` means \"size me to my content\" and a zero base size inside a\ncontent-sized parent collapses it to nothing). Same `flex: 1 1 0` debt as `with_share`, documented as\nsuch — **F004/P006/T010 still replaces both with one `share(n)` setter.**\n\nTests measure pixels, not flags (the T021 lesson): shares still divide 296/296 through the wrappers; a\ncontent-sized container keeps its 120px; the focus ring is found in the painted scene and disappears\nwhen focus moves elsewhere (same tree, one signal); the dock letter is stamped over the dock; focus\nsurvives a `move_container`; no navigable dock ⇒ nothing to focus.\n\n## Left open, on purpose\n\n- **Interaction** — the whole of P085(F007).\n- A click that focuses an *arbitrary* dock: the mouse layer is still region-named\n  (`MouseLeftSidebar` / `mouse/surface_left.rs`), so only the sidebar-nav click path focuses (via\n  `SidebarFocus`). P085(F007)/T354 removes that naming.\n- Only one container was on screen, so \"a letter per dock\" and \"focus moving between docks\" were not\n  demonstrable. P085(F007)/T359 seats the second one.\n\nWorkspace green (1009 tests, was 985), clippy 0 warnings.",
      "notes": "",
