    {
      "id": "42fc5b3c-1724-4b51-9a5e-cc4370da653b",
      "phaseId": "99d19246-1ba9-4720-9895-e70c66acf144",
      "number": 348,
      "shortId": "3F5LR",
      "priority": 21,
      "shortName": "each-container-scrolls-its-own",
      "title": "Containers scroll by nesting their own scroll area; fractional heights",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "# The model (from the user, 2026-07-27)\n\nA **scroll area is a container**. It can be nested anywhere — including inside another container, for\nwhatever widgets that one holds. So scrolling is **composed**, not a property some layer owns:\n\n```\n┌─ sidebar (a shell) ─┐\n│ ┌─────────────────┐ │\n│ │ container 1     │ │  ½ height, nests its own scroll area\n│ └─────────────────┘ │\n│ ┌─────────────────┐ │\n│ │ container 2     │ │  ½ height, nests its own scroll area\n│ └─────────────────┘ │\n└─────────────────────┘\n```\n\n**My earlier framing of this task was wrong** and is corrected here: it said the shell must stop\nscrolling and its scroll area must be *moved* into the container. Nothing is misplaced. A shell may\nhave one, a container inside it may have its own, and nesting already decides which acts — the\ninnermost that *can* scroll the axis wins, the outer gets it only when the inner declines. The wheel\nworks that way today and the keyboard intents shipped in `096f06e` follow the same order.\n\n# What is actually missing\n\n**No container nests a scroll area, so they all share the shell's.** `build_region_content`\n(`heca/src/chrome/mod.rs:1464`) stacks every contribution into one `Flex::column().gap(8.0)`\n(`:1481-1489`) and `build_sidebar_shell` wraps the stack in the shell's single `ScrollRegion`\n(`:1541`) — the only one in the app crate. Two docks in a sidebar therefore scroll as one column:\nscrolling the first slides the second off the bottom. That is a gap in composition, not a\nmisplacement.\n\n# ✅ DECIDED BY THE USER 2026-07-27\n\n- **Heights are fractional, never fixed.** A container in a shell takes a percentage / fraction of\n  the available height (`½` and `½` for two), so it has bounds to scroll *within* and the split\n  survives any window size. No px heights.\n- **The offset's name must be generic.** `SharedChromeState.workspaces.scroll`\n  (`heca/src/chrome/state.rs:168`) is wrong twice over: it is a **region-level** field named after a\n  **single dock**, for a scroll area that belongs to the shell. Whatever nests a scroll area owns its\n  own offset — key it by the container (or region) generically, never by a dock's name.\n- **The right sidebar is the test bed, and it is temporary.** Put **two containers in the right\n  sidebar, ½ / ½ height, each with its own scroll area, each holding the same workspaces dock content\n  as the left** — real content that overflows, so independent scrolling can actually be seen. Once it\n  is verified working, **the right sidebar goes back to empty**; its real features come later. Do not\n  leave the test bed shipped.\n\n# Detail to settle while building\n\nThe test bed wants the workspaces content **twice**. A provider is registered once and seated once,\nand ids are unique, so decide how: two mounted instances with distinct ids, or the same container body\nbuilt twice for the test. Prefer whichever does not require special-casing the host — and if it does\nrequire it, say so rather than bending the host for a temporary test bed.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. A container's body can nest its own scroll area, with its own offset, keyed generically.\n2. Rename/rehome the shell's offset off `workspaces.scroll` to something generic.\n3. Fractional height distribution for containers sharing a shell.\n4. The right-sidebar test bed: two containers, ½ / ½, both scrolling, both holding the workspaces\n   content.\n5. **USER looks at it** — two scroll areas moving independently, and the split holding at different\n   window sizes. This is layout; tests are not verification.\n6. Strip the test bed once verified, leaving the right sidebar empty.\n\n# Then\n\n- **F003/P011/T020** (chrome keyboard focus) targets a **container**, not a side and not a region.\n- **F003/P011/T012**'s app half routes scroll intents to the focused container. Its widget half needs\n  no change: `ScrollRegion::keyboard_target` is per instance, so it fits a nested area as readily as a\n  shell's.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- Two containers in one sidebar scroll independently, with fractional heights.\n- No scroll offset named after a dock.\n- The right sidebar ends up empty again.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\n# ✅ DONE 2026-07-28 — verified in the app by the user\n\nScrolling is **composed**: a scroll area is a container, nested where scrolling belongs. Commits\n`1f4b92d` `7b3a624` `fd403dd` `1d75cb3` `3084714` `8c65a54` `fcbba56` `95a894d`.\n\n- **Every scroll area owns its offset, per placement.** Three were tangled into one field: the left\n  shell used the workspaces *container's* offset, the right shell had none and forgot its position on\n  every rebuild, and the container's own was keyed by container *kind* — so two placements would have\n  scrolled together, invisible until there were two. Offsets are now keyed by **mount id** on the shared\n  store, created on first ask so they outlive the retained tree. `WorkspacesScrollChanged` became\n  `ContainerScrollChanged`, carrying which container moved.\n- **The shell does not scroll.** A viewport measures content at natural height, so wrapping the stack\n  left the containers content-sized (a share had nothing to divide) and took the wheel for the sidebar\n  instead of the container under the cursor. `RegionState.scroll` and the shell's `scroll` parameter\n  were added and then deleted in the same session — the real answer was that the shell should not\n  scroll, not that it needed an offset of its own.\n- **A container declares its share** as `grow` on the contribution, default `1.0`. Named `grow`, not\n  `height_grow`, because `flex_grow` is main-axis relative — one number is height in a sidebar and\n  width in a bar. The region applies it, since a share only means something relative to siblings a\n  container cannot see.\n- **A share had to divide the region, not the leftovers.** `flex_grow` distributes only positive free\n  space, so two containers measured **1214px each inside a 600px body**. With a zero base size and\n  permission to shrink (CSS `flex: 1 1 0`): **296px each**. Debt recorded — expressing a proportion by\n  writing a fixed measure is wrong, and `Layout` has no `flex_basis` (**F004/P006/T010**).\n- **Placing a container twice is two calls.** `WorkspacesContainerProvider::placed(id, region)`\n  replaced a hardcoded id and default region; `BuildCx` carries the mount id, built per container\n  rather than per region, so one plain `fn` still serves every placement and per-placement state has\n  somewhere to live.\n- **A rule between containers**, taking no share of the height (292 / 1 / 291 measured).\n- **`margin_x` / `margin_y`** added for parity with padding, so a described tree can set a margin by\n  axis instead of naming both sides.\n\n**Verified by the user**: two containers half and half, scrolling independently, each its own\nscrollbar. It took three attempts, and the test that passed in between asserted a `grow` *flag* while\nthe layout was visibly broken — the replacement measures pixels.\n\n**Docs (requested explicitly):** `docs/widgets.md` gained container shares, scrolling-by-nesting and\n`ScrollRegion`'s keyboard intents; the README states the model — widget / container / shell, left and\nright being only position.\n\n**The test bed is gone and stays gone** (`95a894d`, user decision 2026-07-28). The right sidebar is\nempty until it has real features. Consequence for T012/T020: only one container is on screen, so a\nsingle container is all that can be demonstrated — say so plainly rather than implying more.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
