    {
      "id": "e73685a2-4e0e-4287-965a-796c64ee8f2d",
      "phaseId": "35d74cc9-a3df-49c9-a5bb-59e12a88b49c",
      "number": 349,
      "shortId": "Q2MRR",
      "priority": 7,
      "shortName": "resize-handle-a-widget-between",
      "title": "Resize handle: a widget between two siblings that drags the split",
      "status": "planned",
      "description": "# What it is\n\nA **handle placed between two containers** that lets the user drag the split between them —\nvertically, horizontally, or either, according to the axis it sits on — **and the same split moved\nfrom the keyboard** through the resize mode. Requested by the user 2026-07-28, after two containers\nsharing a sidebar landed (F003/P011/T021).\n\n# Where it goes and what it replaces\n\n**A widget in `heca-grid-ui`, not app code.** The app hand-rolls this for its dividers today:\n`heca/src/mouse/resize.rs` (`mod resize` at `heca/src/mouse.rs:14`) tracks a divider drag,\n`mouse.rs:30-42` gates DnD and focus-follow while one is in flight, and `resize::cursor_for`\n(`mouse.rs:58`) picks the OS cursor for the divider under the pointer. It emits a **parameterized\nresize action** through the registry rather than mutating state directly — the right shape, worth\nkeeping.\n\n**Absorb, don't duplicate:** decide explicitly whether `mouse/resize.rs` becomes a thin caller of the\nwidget or is deleted. Leaving both is the failure this task exists to avoid. `RegionState.size`\n(`heca/src/chrome/state.rs`) plus the existing action already persist a sidebar's width — that\npersistence model is the precedent to follow.\n\n# What it must sit between\n\n- **`VStack` / `HStack`** — a handle adjusts two siblings on the stack's axis.\n- **A region's container stack** — `build_region_content` (`heca/src/chrome/mod.rs`) stacks containers\n  and interleaves a `Separator`; a handle is the draggable form of that rule.\n- Check what else genuinely applies: `Grid` (tracks, not siblings — a different problem),\n  `Pane`/`PaneFrame`, and the sidebar↔content divider resized today.\n\n# ⌨️ The resize mode — ✅ DECIDED BY THE USER 2026-07-28\n\nThe mode **already exists** and is config-defined, not an `InputMode` variant (an earlier note in this\ntask claimed there was no resize mode — wrong, and that is why: looking at the enum misses it):\n\n```toml\n# keybindings.default.toml:329 — prefix+r → sticky resize mode\n[[keys.mode]]\nname = \"resize\"\ntrigger = \"prefix+r\"\nsticky = true                                   # stays until Esc\n[[keys.mode.bindings]]\naction = \"resize\"\nkeys = \"h\"\nargs = { target = \"column\", axis = \"x\", amount = \"-50\" }\n```\n\nA mode is a **sub-keymap**: while active its bindings apply instead of the normal ones. Built-ins are\n`resize`, `sidebar`, `selection` (`:291`), and the blocks work in `config.toml` too — both files merge\nbefore being read.\n\nThe decisions:\n\n1. **It ships as a default app keybinding and stays customizable.** `prefix+r` is the shipped default;\n   every binding inside the mode stays overridable in `config.toml`. The **container bindings are\n   defaults too** — not something a user must add to get the feature.\n2. **Any resizable area, both axes.** Not columns and panes only: a container, a region, two siblings\n   of a stack — anything the handle can sit between. `ResizeTarget` (`heca/src/input.rs:18`, today\n   `Column | Pane`, `EnumArg::VALUES = [\"column\", \"col\", \"pane\"]`) grows variants; `axis` already\n   covers x and y through `ResizeAxis`. Vertical **and** horizontal for every target that has both.\n3. **The keybinding file declares a FIXED amount; the proportion is worked out internally, per\n   target.** The config keeps writing plain numbers exactly as it does now (`\"-50\"`, `\"40\"`) — that unit\n   does **not** change, so **no config migrates and nothing a user has written breaks**. What changes\n   is inside: the handler converts that fixed step into a **proportional** change for the target it was\n   given, and what gets stored is the proportion. So a resize behaves the same on a small window and a\n   large one, and a container's split survives a window resize because it moved shares (`flex_grow`,\n   F003/P011/T021) rather than pixels.\n   - One conversion, in one place, taking the target + its current extent + the fixed amount. The drag\n     and the key must both go through it; two conversions will drift.\n   - The knowledge of what a step means for a given target lives with the target, which is why this is\n     \"per target\" and not a constant.\n4. **A binding may pass its own amount, or leave it out for the default.** `amount` becomes an\n   **optional** argument with a documented default, so the shipped bindings stay terse and a user who\n   wants coarser steps writes a number. Side effect worth knowing: with no required args left, `resize`\n   becomes legal as a *bare* binding too — the rule that blocked that was about required arguments\n   (F003/P010/T006).\n\nOther resize actions for reference: `resize_to` (absolute, `actions.rs:1832`), `resize_increase` /\n`resize_decrease` (bare, `prefix+=` / `prefix+-`, **column-specific**), `resize_column_by` /\n`resize_pane_height_by` (`:2000`, `:2012`). Ask whether they route through the same conversion, and if\nnot, say why in their descriptors.\n\n## Which area it acts on\n\n\"The focused one\" needs **F003/P011/T020** (`FocusDock` — chrome keyboard focus as a container id).\nUntil that lands a binding names its target explicitly, which mode bindings do anyway since they carry\nargs.\n\n# What a drag changes\n\nA drag moves the **shares** of its two neighbours, never px sizes — through the same conversion the\nkeyboard uses. Work out:\n\n- how a pixel delta becomes a change in two grow factors (the pair's sum is the invariant);\n- the floor: an area must not be draggable or keyable to zero — a minimum, and whether it snaps shut;\n- where it persists: per **placement**, keyed by container id in `SharedChromeState`, like the scroll\n  offsets, so it outlives a tree rebuild and two placements resize independently.\n\n# Also\n\n- **Axis from the parent's direction** where possible rather than the caller restating it; explicit\n  override for the two-axis case.\n- **Cursor feedback** already exists for dividers (`resize::cursor_for`) — reuse it.\n- **Declarative surface** comes free via `#[prop]` + the drift guard, so a described tree can carry a\n  resizable split. The drag callback is host-only, like every callback.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- One implementation: the app's divider resizing goes through the widget, or is gone.\n- A handle between two areas drags their split; the split survives a window resize.\n- **`prefix+r` then a key resizes a container** by default, both axes, through the existing mode and\n  the existing `resize` action — and RPC does the same, one code path.\n- The config still declares fixed amounts; **no existing binding changes meaning**. The proportional\n  conversion is internal, one function, shared by drag and key.\n- Nothing can be reduced to nothing.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.\n- **The USER drags it and keys it in the app** — interaction, so tests are not verification.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
